PixelHeat is a native Android app (Kotlin, Jetpack Compose, Material 3 Expressive, Navigation 3, Room, WorkManager,
a foreground monitoring service, Shizuku/root shell access, minSdk 34).
Before working on one of these topics, read the matching SKILL.md in `.agent-skills/` and follow it:

- Material 3 Expressive look, tokens, shapes, motion springs: `m3-expressive`
- Compose animations (springs, transitions, shared elements): `compose-animations`
- Composable API design, slots, modifiers, previews: `compose-component-design`
- State hoisting, effects, collectAsStateWithLifecycle, ViewModel state: `compose-state-and-effects`
- Recomposition, stability, jank in charts/lists, deferred state reads: `compose-performance`, `jetpack-compose-performance`
- Navigation 3 back stack, entries, ViewModel scoping: `navigation-3`
- System bars, insets, edge-to-edge: `edge-to-edge`
- APK size, R8, keep rules: `r8-analyzer`
- ANR, main-thread work, jank: `anr-audit-and-fix`, `android-performance-anr-and-jank-prevention`
- Memory leaks: `android-performance-memory-leak-prevention`
- Foreground service, WorkManager, process death, background limits: `state-and-background-footguns`
- Runtime permissions, exported components, boot receiver, intents, AIDL/Binder exposure: `android-permissions-security`, `android-intent-security`
- Profiling with Perfetto and the Android profiler: `android-profiler`

Ignore advice in these skills about XML Views or Media3 playback unless the task needs it.
No skill covers Room, Shizuku or root shells: follow the existing code in data/db and privilege, and check official docs for API details.
Keep the app lightweight and battery-friendly: it samples thermal data in the background.